The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix. Its own computed cause table contradicts that claim. This review splits the exported reports by the desk's executable classifier, gives each cause its own ticket, and proposes a safer intake rule. It does not establish that either product bug has been fixed.

## What the export shows

<SplitSummary />

These tables recompute from the report export and tenant directory whenever the KB is built. The rendered page is a snapshot of the supplied 2026-10-04 export, not a live connection to the desk. Replace the export and rebuild to include new reports; reconcile the GitHub issue counts at the same time.

## Problem A · shared parser reads month first

The shared parser assumes month/day order while day-first tenants write day/month. A date such as 07/04 can become 4 July instead of 7 April. This cause covers app and email reports and form reports whose tenant does not use the month-first booking-v3 hint. It spans both booking-form versions; a version alone is not the grouping rule.

For example, R-41200 is an app report from harbour.dental (en-GB, booking-v2): a 7 April reminder became 4 July. R-41201 corrects the direction in the reporter's account; it is a different report ID, not an export duplicate. Preserve that correction and verify against actual booking values when reproducing.

Fix the parser to use an explicit tenant date order or an unambiguous structured date. Test ambiguous and unambiguous dates across supported tenant locales and channels. Validate the stored booking, reminder and invite date together. A parser change alone does not repair the form's misleading hint.

## Problem B · booking-v3 tells day-first users to type month first

The booking-v3 hint prints MM/DD/YYYY for day-first tenants, while that form interprets input in the tenant's day-first order. Following the hint therefore swaps month and day. This is a separate form contract failure, not another browser label for Problem A.

The affected form reports belong to alder.physio (de-DE), copper.studio (es-ES), and pale.harbour (pt-PT). R-41230 is copper.studio via form, tagged parser at intake but classified hint by the tenant and channel. R-41241 is alder.physio via form with browser locale en-US: the tenant remains de-DE. R-41251 is pale.harbour via form, also tagged parser. These examples show why the browser cannot decide the cause.

Align the displayed hint, validation, parser and confirmation with the tenant's locale. Test a user following the displayed hint, including ambiguous dates, for each affected tenant. Retest booking-v2 and app/email paths independently. Track and verify this fix on its own ticket.

## Symptoms that need another look

The desk notes flag the reports listed above as reading like a one-day move. Their bodies include a weekday shift, June-only shifts, an invite arriving before booking, and Lisbon/Toronto seeing different days. The supplied classifier places them in parser by tenant and channel; the code does not prove that their symptom has the same underlying cause.

Keep them in the parser count, footnote the uncertainty, and ask for the original input, stored timestamp, tenant and client time zones, DST context, and invite payload. A third root cause is not established by this export. If investigation confirms one, move those report IDs to a separate ticket and reconcile both counts without double counting.

## The desk is still working

